{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "204",
    "case_summary": "A 17-year-old single Turkish woman presented with a 3-day history of nausea, vomiting, and right lower-quadrant abdominal pain. She had no known exposure to tuberculosis. On examination she was afebrile with tender right lower abdominal quadrant and no rebound. Initial abdominal and chest radiographs were normal, and a bedside abdominal ultrasound showed no abnormalities. Laboratory studies revealed a leukocyte count of 12,200/μL, C-reactive protein of 12 mg/L, blood urea nitrogen of 35 mg/dL, and creatinine of 1.02 mg/dL. Urinalysis was unremarkable and a urine human chorionic gonadotropin test was negative. HIV testing was negative. While under observation, a second abdominopelvic ultrasound was interpreted as suggestive of retrocecal appendicitis. The patient was taken to the operating room via a McBurney’s incision. A 15 × 6 cm cystic mass with regular margins was found arising from the right tubo-ovarian complex; the appendix appeared clinically normal. The mass was excised intact, the tubo-ovarian structures were preserved, and on incision of the cyst wall purulent fluid was noted. Cultures were deferred until after the weekend.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. genital tuberculosis\nB. Acute appendicitis was excluded because “the patient's appendix was clinically normal\nC. Ruptured ectopic pregnancy was excluded because “the results of a urine human chorionic gonadotropin assay were negative\nD. Ovarian torsion was unlikely because “an abdominal ultrasound was also normal\nE. A pyogenic PID\nF. Actinomycosis was considered among granulomatous causes but was unlikely in the absence of an intra\nG. Genital tuberculosis was confirmed by characteristic caseating granulomas and a strongly positive tuberculin skin test\nH. None\n",
